  "live_records": {
    "q-162670": {
      "dom_id": "step-q162670",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Suppose we would like to find the maximum of the function $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}z$ on the intersection of the surfaces ${x}^{2}-2{z}^{2}=1$ and ${y}^{2}+3{z}^{3}=5$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨{y}^{2}z,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,x{y}^{2}⟩=\\lambda ⟨2x,\\,0,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩+\\mu ⟨0,\\,2y,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩ \\\\ {x}^{2}-2{z}^{2}=1 \\\\ {y}^{2}+3{z}^{3}=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the expressions?",
      "worked_solution": "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}z\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-2{z}^{2}=1,\\,h(x,y,z)={y}^{2}+3{z}^{3}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=1 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨{y}^{2}z,\\,2xyz,\\,x{y}^{2}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,\\,0,\\,-4z⟩+\\mu ⟨0,\\,2y,\\,9{z}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
