      "source_example_id": "e-12459",
      "knowledge_point": "Constructing a Lagrange System With Two Constraints",
      "kp_id": "e-12459"
    },
    "q-162814": {
      "dom_id": "step-q162814",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Suppose we would like to find the minimum of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}$ on the intersection of the surfaces ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4$ and $2x-y+{z}^{2}=4$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨2x,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,-2z⟩=\\lambda ⟨0,\\,2y,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩+\\mu ⟨2,\\,-1,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩ \\\\ {y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4 \\\\ 2x-y+{z}^{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the expressions?",
      "worked_solution": "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)={y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4,\\,h(x,y,z)=2x-y+{z}^{2}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=4 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,\\,4y,\\,-2z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨0,\\,2y,\\,2z⟩+\\mu ⟨2,\\,-1,\\,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
