    "q-162816": {
      "dom_id": "step-q162816",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Suppose we would like to find the maximum of the function $f(x,y,z)=3x+2z+{y}^{2}$ on the intersection of the surfaces ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=1$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨3,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,2⟩=\\lambda ⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩+\\mu ⟨4x,\\,2y,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩ \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the expressions?",
      "worked_solution": "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=3x+2z+{y}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,\\,h(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=1 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨3,\\,2y,\\,2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,0⟩+\\mu ⟨4x,\\,2y,\\,8z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
