    "q-162894": {
      "dom_id": "step-q162894",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=x+2y+3z$ on the intersection of the plane $x-y-z=0$ and the cylinder $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x+2y+3z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x-y-z=0,\\,h(x,y,z)=4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=0 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,2,3⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,-1,-1⟩+\\mu ⟨8x,2y,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}1=\\lambda +8\\mu x \\\\ 2=-\\lambda +2\\mu y \\\\ 3=-\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{2\\mu } \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2\\mu } \\\\ \\lambda =-3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction.\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{\\mu }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =1 \\\\ {\\mu }^{2} & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ \\mu  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\,y=∓\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $y$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}$ and $y=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y-z & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}-(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}})-z & =0 \\\\ z & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}$ and $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y-z & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}-(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}})-z & =0 \\\\ z & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}),\\,(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})$ | $(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $\\sqrt{5}$ | $-\\sqrt{5}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}),$ and its   maximum value is $\\sqrt{5}.$",
