    "q-162893": {
      "dom_id": "step-q162893",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=4x+y+2z$ on the intersection of the plane $x+y+z=0$ and the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$",
      "worked_solution": "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=4x+y+2z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x+y+z=0,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=0 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,1,2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,1⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,2y,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}4=\\lambda +2\\mu x \\\\ 1=\\lambda +2\\mu y \\\\ 2=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{\\mu } \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2\\mu } \\\\ \\lambda =2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction.\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{\\mu }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =5 \\\\ {\\mu }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\mu  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=\\pm 2,\\,y=∓1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $y$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=2$ and $y=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =0 \\\\ 2+(-1)+z & =0 \\\\ 1+z & =0 \\\\ z & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=-2$ and $y=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =0 \\\\ -2+1+z & =0 \\\\ -1+z & =0 \\\\ z & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(2,-1,-1),\\,(-2,1,1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(2,-1,-1)$ | $(-2,1,1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $5$ | $-5$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-2,1,1),$ and its minimum value is $-5.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
