    "q-162897": {
      "dom_id": "step-q162897",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=2x+4y-2z$ on the intersection of the plane $2x-4y-2z=1$ and the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2.$",
      "worked_solution": "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=2x+4y-2z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=2x-4y-2z=1,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=1 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,4,-2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2,-4,-2⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,0,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2=2\\lambda +2\\mu x \\\\ 4=-4\\lambda  \\\\ -2=-2\\lambda +2\\mu z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{2}{\\mu } \\\\ \\lambda =-1 \\\\ z=-\\frac{2}{\\mu }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction.\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $z$ into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{4}{{\\mu }^{2}}+\\frac{4}{{\\mu }^{2}} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{8}{{\\mu }^{2}} & =2 \\\\ {\\mu }^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=\\pm 1,\\,z=∓1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $z$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=1$ and $z=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-4y-2z & =1 \\\\ 2(1)-4y-2(-1) & =1 \\\\ -4y & =-3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=-1$ and $z=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-4y-2z & =1 \\\\ 2(-1)-4y-2(1) & =1 \\\\ -4y & =5 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(1,\\frac{3}{4},-1),\\,(-1,-\\frac{5}{4},1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(1,\\frac{3}{4},-1)$ | $(-1,-\\frac{5}{4},1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $7$ | $-9$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-1,-\\frac{5}{4},1),$ and its minimum value is $-9.$",
