    "q-162830": {
      "dom_id": "step-q162830",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=4x+2y-z$ on the intersection of the plane $x-y-z=2$ and the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2.$",
      "worked_solution": "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=4x+2y-z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x-y-z=2,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=2 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,2,-1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,-1,-1⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,2y,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}4=\\lambda +2\\mu x \\\\ 2=-\\lambda +2\\mu y \\\\ -1=-\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{3}{2\\mu } \\\\ y=\\frac{3}{2\\mu } \\\\ \\lambda =1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction.\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{9}{4{\\mu }^{2}}+\\frac{9}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2{\\mu }^{2}} & =2 \\\\ {\\mu }^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ \\mu  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, since $x=y,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)=(1,1)\\,\\text{or}\\,(x,y)=(-1,-1).\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $y$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=1$ and $y=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y-z & =2 \\\\ 1-1-z & =2 \\\\ z & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=-1$ and $y=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y-z & =2 \\\\ -1-(-1)-z & =2 \\\\ -1+1-z & =2 \\\\ z & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(1,1,-2),\\,(-1,-1,-2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(1,1,-2)$ | $(-1,-1,-2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $8$ | $-4$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(1,1,-2),$ and its maximum value is $8.$",
